**W3-D3-Models**

---

**What and Why:**
Model = database table defined as a Python class. Instead of writing raw SQL to create tables, Django lets you write Python classes — Django converts them to actual database tables automatically. This is the core of Django — your entire AI Job Portal and SaaS Expense Manager are built on models. Get this solid — every project feature starts with a model.

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: CREATE YOUR FIRST MODEL
# ============================================================

# Open blog/models.py and write this:

from django.db import models
# models = Django's module containing all field types


class Post(models.Model):
    # Post inherits from models.Model
    # models.Model = Django's base class for all database tables
    # class Post = one database table called 'blog_post'
    # Django automatically adds 'blog_' prefix + app name

    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
    # CharField = short text field (like VARCHAR in SQL)
    # max_length=200 = maximum 200 characters allowed
    # required — CharField must have max_length

    content = models.TextField()
    # TextField = long text field (like TEXT in SQL)
    # no max_length needed — stores unlimited text
    # use for blog content, descriptions, long text

    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)
    # DateTimeField = stores date + time
    # auto_now_add=True = automatically saves current date/time
    # when object is CREATED — never changes after that
    # you never set this manually — Django handles it

    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)
    # auto_now=True = automatically updates to current date/time
    # every time object is SAVED/UPDATED
    # different from auto_now_add — updates every save

    is_published = models.BooleanField(default=False)
    # BooleanField = True or False only
    # default=False = every new post starts as unpublished
    # you set to True when ready to publish

    def __str__(self):
        return self.title
        # controls how Post object shows in admin panel and shell
        # without this: shows <Post object (1)> — useless
        # with this: shows actual title — "My First Post"
```

